  "slug": "dna-binding-6305",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers address RNA-seq pipeline QC, large-scale normalization, sample size planning, and non-fixed scRNA-seq assay benchmarking.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the analytical reliability of bulk and single-cell RNA-seq data, addressed through quality control, normalization, study design, and assay comparison. QC-DR provides a reference-based framework for visualizing pipeline QC metrics and flagging outlier bulk samples, while RUVprps offers faster RUV-III normalization using pseudo-replicates of pseudo-samples for large datasets, together with assessment metrics. The sample size study addresses design, estimating median requirements of 190 to 480 samples for machine learning binary classification in bulk RNA-seq across XGBoost, random forest, and neural networks. The single-cell preprint benchmarks four non-formalin-fixed scRNA-seq assays (10x, Parse, Scale, Illumina) on human PBMCs using both agnostic and platform-specific cell calling. What is new is the head-to-head comparison of cell-calling strategies across fixation-free single-cell platforms, set alongside normalization and QC tools aimed at scale.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
